labour shadow business secretary  jonathan reynolds is uh here with us how  embarrassed are you that we've only  allowed 50 refugees into this country  very i think  we were receiving  very good intelligence reports weeks  before russia actually invaded people  were aware of that there was the  conversation about that so i can't  understand  the lack of planning on the humanitarian  side that the government just seems to  to have not done it and of course  let's all be clear so we're all i think  incredibly heartened  by their ukrainian resistance but  originally people thought ukraine could  fall within 48 hours so of course the  humanitarian flaws would have been  expected to be even greater than what we  have seen the government need to do an  emergency  visa  emergency protection visa this is this  is not business as usual this is not  normal immigration to the uk they should  have a special regime in place  recognizing that these people coming  from ukraine are not in a traditional  they can get visa application  is true i mean they're not needing visas  to go into poland well i understand that  because of the eu what i would say is  i've seen in the past as a member of  parliament how this country sometimes  deals with people coming to it it hasn't  always been very good there hasn't  always been a good distribution of  people around the uk and the support for  them that they require so i would want  to know how many people are coming to  the uk so we can give them the support  that is required especially children  fleeing a war zone but that doesn't have  to be a bureaucratic system that keeps  people out you could do that in a very  straightforward way that would give  people humanitarian assistance they need  in a way that's not happening at the  moment but i must put it to you that  labour is not doing enough to try to  force the government's hand on this  families are having cluster bombs  dropped on them in europe as we speak  we should be doing more but we should be  doing more as a country and we're doing  what we can in parliament not to make it  a party political issue but to make and  advocate for what needs to happen so  that's why we said that emergency  protection visa should be a crucial part  of that on all of these things we want  to see the government do the right thing  we don't be attacking them just for the  sake of it but where they are failing  obviously it's our duty to point that  out and we will do that what you have  done is let the government get away  quite a long time with this with not  putting the economic crime bill um into  effect i was looking at some uh figures  um from uh a barrister who said as far  back as 2018 laws are in place where  sanctions could have been uh put in  place against these oligarchs already so  why have they not been yeah well you're  absolutely right to say this is the  central argument of the economic crime  bill today the government have agreed  with us on several things but the  crucial question is how long is  reasonable to expect foreign companies  to register the true ownership of  property in the uk the government said  18 months originally they've come down  to six months we're seeing 28 days and  the reason why i think that is  reasonable is exactly what you've just  said david cameron promised this in 2016  the process began in 2018 it should have  been well in force over a year ago so  i'm not just saying really 28 days i'm  saying it's 28 days plus the six years  where this has already been announced  and that's why i want to see this action  taken now it's not just about oligarchs  it's about money launders it's about tax  evasion this black hole of transparency  on this issue has facilitated misery  crime and in many cases people being  able to loot the wealth of their country  and stash it here in the uk that's  what's got to change today and what  about the entities and individuals that  have so far been sanctioned by the  government i'm a bit confused about the  numbers on that well  what we are clearly seeing which is what  everyone is asking why are there people  who have been sanctioned by the u.s been  sanctioned by the eu but haven't been  sanctioned by the uk now part of the  answer to that is another part of the  bill this emergency bill that we're  going to support the government on to  get it through with hopefully the  strength and protections in place i  think some of it is clearly that the  government's previous sanctions regime  which was the act in 2018  hasn't worked it's clearly not working  if there's that differential in numbers  i think some of its capacity i think we  haven't taken this area of of the  capacity the country needs as seriously  as we should have done but hopefully  with the amendments that have been  accepted by the government today this  will improve but be in no doubt we've  got to get this right and we've got to  start improving our performance what  will that look like what will that look  like you know for my viewers at home  what are we actually talking about we've  seen yachts being seized off the coast  of france um by the french government  from oligarchs i mean what's going to  happen to i don't know chelsea football  club program well the government seem  concerned by the potential legal  challenge they might get by sanctioning  people if they haven't got a legal  regime that is robust enough and that is  hugely frustrating because they should  have had one in place to clearly deal  with a situation like that another set  of the discussions around this economic  crime bill today will be about whether  it is reasonable to allow people to  divest of their assets to sell it  whether that's a yacht a football club  or property whilst they are the subject  of investigation these are called  unexplained wealth orders they haven't  been working again very well in the past  we've had a couple have yeah  very few relative to the scale of the  problem of course  the crucial thing about this is it's  quite hard sometimes to verify what  really went on in the form of ussr after  the fall of communism a lot of murky  things went on where people ended up in  charge of entire state industries so  that's really about the burden of proof  being on these people to prove their  wealth is legitimate but there are  things that need to be done to change  the regime to make sure that action can  be taken and you're not just simply  giving people a chance to sell their  assets to probably sell them at a  discount but to take the money and run  they've got to be subject to the right  regime yes this regime should have been  in place before today and that's a huge  frustration you might remember a month  ago lord agnew resigned from the  government citing the fact the  government said they weren't even going  to do an economic crime bill this year  for the next 15 months well clearly  that's had to change very substantially  it's going to happen all stages of of  the bill will go through the house of  commons today  we'll facilitate that we think we can  strengthen it if the government work  further with us and other mps do it  would clearly be better if it had  already been in place

